  "allocation": "84 slots (sdc-001..sdc-084) in fixed corpus order: phase 0 (12), phase 1 (24), phase 2 (24), phase 3 (24); within each phase, seeded slots precede controls. 30 seeded = 6 types (CV,OO,LC,FT,HF,RC) x 5 severities each: 1 CRITICAL, 3 MAJOR, 1 MAJOR-preferred (MAJOR preferred; MINOR allowed only when the type genuinely cannot carry MAJOR on that base -- protocol targets MINOR <= 4 overall). Phase 0 carries one plain-MAJOR seed per type (6 total, the newest of each type's 5 assigned bases). Phases 1-3 carry the remaining 4 seeds per type (24 total, 8 per phase): each type's CRITICAL seed lands in exactly one of phases 1-3 (spread 2 types per phase: CV/OO->phase1, LC/FT->phase2, HF/RC->phase3), paired in that same phase with one extra plain-MAJOR seed of the same type; the type's remaining plain-MAJOR seed and MAJOR-preferred seed each land in one of the other two phases so every type appears in every phase at least once (8 = 6 types x1 baseline + 2 types-with-an-extra per phase). Seeded bases: the 36 S/M-size best_candidates.json shortlist entries, sorted newest-first by committer timestamp, assigned round-robin across the 6 types (type = position mod 6); each type's first 5 round-robin picks (newest-first within the type) become its 5 seed bases, mapped newest->oldest to phase0-MAJOR, crit-phase CRITICAL, crit-phase extra-MAJOR, next-phase MAJOR, last-phase MAJOR-preferred; each type's 6th (oldest) pick is unused as a seed and falls to the control pool. The 4 L-size shortlist entries are never seed bases. Controls (54): priority order is (a) the 6 SM shortlist entries left over from the round-robin, plus the 4 L shortlist entries, newest-first (10 total); then (b) full_table non-shortlisted, non-L, kind in {code,mixed} commits, newest-first; then (c) full_table non-shortlisted kind=docs S/M commits, newest-first; taken in that order until 54 and laid into phases 0/1/2/3 at 6/16/16/16 in the same priority order (so phase 0's controls are drawn from the newest of the leftover-shortlist set). The full priority-ordered candidate sequence (leftover-shortlist, then non-L code/mixed, then docs S/M, then -- protocol S:2.1's pool-ran-short allowance -- the 30 used seed bases newest-first) is walked greedily, taking each candidate unless its parent sha (the \"base\") was already claimed by a higher-priority control: one collision was found (e7545f6 and 99835d5 are git siblings sharing parent e3b730d; the newer, higher-priority e7545f6 was kept and 99835d5 was skipped, recorded in README.md). The non-reuse tiers (10 leftover-shortlist minus the 1 collision-skip = 9, + 11 non-L code/mixed + 24 docs S/M = 44 usable) therefore supplied 44 controls, and the remaining 10 are reused seed bases, reviewed unmodified (kind=control) against the same parent already used for that base's seeded variant -- recorded per-sha in README.md. Reuse does not violate the \"no duplicate (base, kind) pair\" check: the pair's kind differs (seeded vs control) even though the base (parent sha) repeats. Variant (V1/V2/V3) rotation per protocol S:2.7, AMENDED for seeds by coordinator ruling (superseding the original per-(type,target_severity)-stratum rule, which always landed V1 on every 1-member stratum and produced an 18/6/6 skew): seeds rotate within type -- each type's 5 seeds, in corpus order, cycle V1,V2,V3,V1,V2 -- with the starting variant staggered per type (offsets 0,1,2,0,1,2 by type order CV,OO,LC,FT,HF,RC: CV starts V1, OO starts V2, LC starts V3, FT starts V1, HF starts V2, RC starts V3), so the 30-seed total comes out 10/10/10 despite 5 not being a multiple of 3. Controls are unaffected: they still rotate as one stratum across all 54 in corpus order (18 each of V1/V2/V3). All shas are full 40-hex, resolved via `git rev-parse <sha>` (commit) and `git rev-parse <sha>^` (base/parent). Exclusion check ran across all 102 full_table.txt rows: 4 commits touch a *-SEALED.md path (68d8171, 6327995, 5fc9726, 6f27ea2 -- WO-7a corpus-sealing commits, none in the shortlist) and 1 is the repo root commit with no parent to diff against (bbbd983); none of the 40 shortlisted commits were excluded, matching the scout's finding. 10 controls reuse a seeded base (see README.md) because the non-reuse control pool, after a 1-item base collision skip (44 usable of 45 available), fell short of the 54 needed.",
